#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503753 :question/problem "Mary wonders where and when they'd meet if Sam left home $3.8$ minutes earlier and walked with a speed of $50$ meters per minute. She finds that the distance $d$ from home they'd meet can be modeled using the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}d+60t &=960 \\\\ d-50t &=190.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time (in minutes) since Mary left the community park.\n\nMary and Sam would meet$\\,$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$minutes after Mary left the community park.\n\nMary and Sam would meet at a distance of $\\,$\n{{field-2}}$\\,$meters from home." :question/worked-solution "To solve the given system of linear equations, we will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. It doesn't matter which equation we pick first, so we choose the easiest one. In this case, the second equation looks like the easiest.\n\nUsing the second equation, we can isolate $d$ adding to both sides of the equation $50t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d-50t &=190 \\\\ d &=190+50t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we replace $d$ with $190+50t$ in the first equation and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d+60t=960 \\\\ (190+50t)+60t & =960 \\\\ 190+110t & =960 \\\\ 110t & =960-190 \\\\ 110t & =770 \\\\ t & =\\frac{770}{110} \\\\ t & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, they would have met after $t=7$ minutes.\n\nIf we substitute $t=7$ into $d=190+50t$, we can find the value of $d:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =190+50t \\\\  & =190+50(7) \\\\  & =540\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, they meet at a distance of $540$ meters from home."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503848 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int {x}^{6}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the power rule with $n=6$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\int {x}^{6}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{6+1}}{6+1}+C=\\frac{{x}^{7}}{7}+C.\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{7}}{7}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{7}}{7}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{7}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{7})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{7}\\cdot 7{x}^{6} \\\\ &= {x}^{6}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503851 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int {x}^{7}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ Include the arbitrary constant of integration." :question/worked-solution "We apply the power rule with $n=7$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\int {x}^{7}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{7+1}}{7+1}+C=\\frac{{x}^{8}}{8}+C.\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{8}}{8}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{8}}{8}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{8}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{8})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{8}\\cdot 8{x}^{7} \\\\ &= {x}^{7}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503854 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int {x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the power rule with $n=5$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\int {x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{5+1}}{5+1}+C=\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6}+C.\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{6}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{6})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 6{x}^{5} \\\\ &= {x}^{5}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503860 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int {x}^{8}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the power rule with $n=8$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\int {x}^{8}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{8+1}}{8+1}+C=\\frac{{x}^{9}}{9}+C.\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{9}}{9}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{9}}{9}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{9}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{9})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{9}\\cdot 9{x}^{8} \\\\ &= {x}^{8}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503871 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the power rule with $n=3$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\int {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{3+1}}{3+1}+C=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+C.\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{4}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{4})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 4{x}^{3} \\\\ &= {x}^{3}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503879 :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of $\\sqrt[2]{{x}^{5}}.$\n\nWork on $x>0$.\n\nThe antiderivative is {{field-1}} $+C$. Enter only the term before $+C$, with no additive constant." :question/worked-solution "We start by writing the root as a rational power, $\\sqrt[2]{{x}^{5}}={x}^{5/2}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{5}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt[2]{{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{5/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5/2+1}}{(\\frac{5}{2}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{7/2}}{\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{7/2}}{7}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{7/2}}{7}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{2{x}^{7/2}}{7}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{7}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{7/2})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{7}\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}{x}^{7/2-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{5/2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503882 :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of ${x}^{3/2}.$\n\nWork on $x>0$.\n\nThe antiderivative is {{field-1}} $+C$. Enter only the term before $+C$, with no additive constant." :question/worked-solution "We can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{3}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{3/2+1}}{(\\frac{3}{2}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5/2}}{\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{5/2}}{5}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{5/2}}{5}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{2{x}^{5/2}}{5}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{5}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{5/2})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{5}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}{x}^{5/2-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{3/2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503887 :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of ${x}^{1/2}.$\n\nWork on $x>0$.\n\nThe antiderivative is {{field-1}} $+C$. Enter only the term before $+C$, with no additive constant." :question/worked-solution "We can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{1}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{1/2+1}}{(\\frac{1}{2}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3/2}}{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3/2})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}{x}^{3/2-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{1/2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503893 :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of $\\sqrt[2]{{x}^{9}}.$\n\nWork on $x>0$.\n\nThe antiderivative is {{field-1}} $+C$. Enter only the term before $+C$, with no additive constant." :question/worked-solution "We start by writing the root as a rational power, $\\sqrt[2]{{x}^{9}}={x}^{9/2}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{9}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt[2]{{x}^{9}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{9/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{9/2+1}}{(\\frac{9}{2}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{11/2}}{\\left(\\frac{11}{2}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{11/2}}{11}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{11/2}}{11}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{2{x}^{11/2}}{11}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{11}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{11/2})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{11}\\cdot \\frac{11}{2}{x}^{11/2-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{9/2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503894 :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of $\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{7}}.$\n\nWork on $x>0$.\n\nThe antiderivative is {{field-1}} $+C$. Enter only the term before $+C$, with no additive constant." :question/worked-solution "We start by writing the root as a rational power, $\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{7}}={x}^{7/5}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{7}{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt[5]{{x}^{7}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{7/5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{7/5+1}}{(\\frac{7}{5}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{12/5}}{\\left(\\frac{12}{5}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5{x}^{12/5}}{12}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5{x}^{12/5}}{12}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{5{x}^{12/5}}{12}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{12}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{12/5})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{12}\\cdot \\frac{12}{5}{x}^{12/5-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{7/5}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503896 :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of ${x}^{2/3}.$\n\nWork on $x>0$.\n\nThe antiderivative is {{field-1}} $+C$. Enter only the term before $+C$, with no additive constant." :question/worked-solution "We can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{2}{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2/3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{2/3+1}}{(\\frac{2}{3}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5/3}}{\\left(\\frac{5}{3}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{5/3}}{5}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{3{x}^{5/3}}{5}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{3{x}^{5/3}}{5}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{3}{5}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{5/3})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{3}{5}\\cdot \\frac{5}{3}{x}^{5/3-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{2/3}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503897 :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of $\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{6}}.$\n\nWork on $x>0$.\n\nThe antiderivative is {{field-1}} $+C$. Enter only the term before $+C$, with no additive constant." :question/worked-solution "We start by writing the root as a rational power, $\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{6}}={x}^{6/5}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{6}{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt[5]{{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{6/5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{6/5+1}}{(\\frac{6}{5}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{11/5}}{\\left(\\frac{11}{5}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5{x}^{11/5}}{11}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5{x}^{11/5}}{11}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{5{x}^{11/5}}{11}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{11}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{11/5})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{11}\\cdot \\frac{11}{5}{x}^{11/5-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{6/5}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503900 :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of $\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{9}}.$\n\nWork on $x>0$.\n\nThe antiderivative is {{field-1}} $+C$. Enter only the term before $+C$, with no additive constant." :question/worked-solution "We start by writing the root as a rational power, $\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{9}}={x}^{9/5}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{9}{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt[5]{{x}^{9}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{9/5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{9/5+1}}{(\\frac{9}{5}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{14/5}}{\\left(\\frac{14}{5}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5{x}^{14/5}}{14}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5{x}^{14/5}}{14}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{5{x}^{14/5}}{14}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{14}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{14/5})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{14}\\cdot \\frac{14}{5}{x}^{14/5-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{9/5}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503902 :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of $\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{8}}.$\n\nWork on $x>0$.\n\nThe antiderivative is {{field-1}} $+C$. Enter only the term before $+C$, with no additive constant." :question/worked-solution "We start by writing the root as a rational power, $\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{8}}={x}^{8/5}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{8}{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt[5]{{x}^{8}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{8/5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{8/5+1}}{(\\frac{8}{5}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{13/5}}{\\left(\\frac{13}{5}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5{x}^{13/5}}{13}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5{x}^{13/5}}{13}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{5{x}^{13/5}}{13}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{13}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{13/5})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{13}\\cdot \\frac{13}{5}{x}^{13/5-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{8/5}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503904 :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of $\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{4}}.$\n\nWork on $x>0$.\n\nThe antiderivative is {{field-1}} $+C$. Enter only the term before $+C$, with no additive constant." :question/worked-solution "We start by writing the root as a rational power, $\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{4}}={x}^{4/5}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{4}{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt[5]{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{4/5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4/5+1}}{(\\frac{4}{5}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{9/5}}{\\left(\\frac{9}{5}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5{x}^{9/5}}{9}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5{x}^{9/5}}{9}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{5{x}^{9/5}}{9}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{9}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{9/5})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{9}\\cdot \\frac{9}{5}{x}^{9/5-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{4/5}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503906 :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of $\\sqrt[2]{{x}^{3}}.$\n\nWork on $x>0$.\n\nThe antiderivative is {{field-1}} $+C$. Enter only the term before $+C$, with no additive constant." :question/worked-solution "We start by writing the root as a rational power, $\\sqrt[2]{{x}^{3}}={x}^{3/2}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{3}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt[2]{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3/2+1}}{(\\frac{3}{2}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5/2}}{\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{5/2}}{5}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{5/2}}{5}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{2{x}^{5/2}}{5}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{5}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{5/2})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{5}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}{x}^{5/2-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{3/2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503913 :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of $\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{4}}.$\n\nWork on $x>0$.\n\nThe antiderivative is {{field-1}} $+C$. Enter only the term before $+C$, with no additive constant." :question/worked-solution "We start by writing the root as a rational power, $\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{4}}={x}^{4/3}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{4}{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt[3]{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{4/3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4/3+1}}{(\\frac{4}{3}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{7/3}}{\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{7/3}}{7}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{3{x}^{7/3}}{7}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{3{x}^{7/3}}{7}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{3}{7}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{7/3})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{3}{7}\\cdot \\frac{7}{3}{x}^{7/3-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{4/3}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503914 :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of $\\sqrt[2]{{x}^{7}}.$\n\nWork on $x>0$.\n\nThe antiderivative is {{field-1}} $+C$. Enter only the term before $+C$, with no additive constant." :question/worked-solution "We start by writing the root as a rational power, $\\sqrt[2]{{x}^{7}}={x}^{7/2}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{7}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt[2]{{x}^{7}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{7/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{7/2+1}}{(\\frac{7}{2}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{9/2}}{\\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{9/2}}{9}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{9/2}}{9}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{2{x}^{9/2}}{9}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{9}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{9/2})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{9}\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}{x}^{9/2-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{7/2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503923 :question/problem "![](images/55/55ac7d0e79f21610191ebb3c861bb1a5d8e3eaf3ad625ad121e7f815bdc3c517.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the vector $a$. Which of the following diagrams shows the vector $-\\frac{3}{2}a$?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $-\\frac{3}{2}a$ must be parallel to $a$.\n\nNow, for the vector $-\\frac{3}{2}a$ we have the following:\n\n- The magnitude of $-\\frac{3}{2}a$ is $\\frac{3}{2}$ times the magnitude of $a$.\n- Vectors $a$ and $-\\frac{3}{2}a$ have opposite directions since the factor $-\\frac{3}{2}$ is negative.\n\nThe required vectors are shown below.\n\n![](images/c5/c5ae3d645aaa0a5cae7a8c5ee0b346310591eaf2bc884475c930ceff3f5bdb68.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503931 :question/problem "![](images/96/9668d08ce01afb28e2aa7613eed2ffc37995f5108e0de1a515ad02705c1ae844.png)\n\nThe vector $a$ is depicted above. Which of the following diagrams shows the vectors $2a$ and $-\\frac{3}{2}a$?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that both $2a$ and $-\\frac{3}{2}a$ must be parallel to $a$.\n\nNow, for the vector $2a$ we have the following:\n\n- The magnitude of $2a$ is $2$ times the magnitude of $a$.\n- Vectors $a$ and $2a$ have the same direction since the factor $2$ is positive.\n\nNext, for the vector $-\\frac{3}{2}a$ we have the following:\n\n- The magnitude of $-\\frac{3}{2}a$ is $\\frac{3}{2}$ times the magnitude of $a$.\n- Vectors $a$ and $-\\frac{3}{2}a$ have opposite directions since the factor $-\\frac{3}{2}$ is negative.\n\nThe required vectors are shown below.\n\n![](images/7f/7f63c606fff90d3d867a8b24a50855cc66c0fbb9e97748f429f5a53641f29c67.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503937 :question/problem "![](images/cc/cc3f84b6d1a75844727d07e0fddc1f7cccd0aec76fb150022d0d62123b1bb553.png)\n\nThe vector $a$ is depicted above. Which of the following diagrams shows the vectors $2a$ and $-\\frac{1}{2}a$?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that both $2a$ and $-\\frac{1}{2}a$ must be parallel to $a$.\n\nNow, for the vector $2a$ we have the following:\n\n- The magnitude of $2a$ is $2$ times the magnitude of $a$.\n- Vectors $a$ and $2a$ have the same direction since the factor $2$ is positive.\n\nNext, for the vector $-\\frac{1}{2}a$ we have the following:\n\n- The magnitude of $-\\frac{1}{2}a$ is $\\frac{1}{2}$ times the magnitude of $a$.\n- Vectors $a$ and $-\\frac{1}{2}a$ have opposite directions since the factor $-\\frac{1}{2}$ is negative.\n\nThe required vectors are shown below.\n\n![](images/bc/bcbc4dbb8e224b898bc8f521a739614e88348d75fbea1f372f039d4c7d3682a5.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503940 :question/problem "![](images/cb/cb9a3adfca909938806eff56b7e7eff2c52e40d0884b9ae1213e12d3f33e7029.png)\n\nThe vector $a$ is depicted above. Which of the following diagrams shows the vectors $2a$ and $-\\frac{2}{3}a$?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that both $2a$ and $-\\frac{2}{3}a$ must be parallel to $a$.\n\nNow, for the vector $2a$ we have the following:\n\n- The magnitude of $2a$ is $2$ times the magnitude of $a$.\n- Vectors $a$ and $2a$ have the same direction since the factor $2$ is positive.\n\nNext, for the vector $-\\frac{2}{3}a$ we have the following:\n\n- The magnitude of $-\\frac{2}{3}a$ is $\\frac{2}{3}$ times the magnitude of $a$.\n- Vectors $a$ and $-\\frac{2}{3}a$ have opposite directions since the factor $-\\frac{2}{3}$ is negative.\n\nThe required vectors are shown below.\n\n![](images/f4/f435d3754370fe01575b6d8e37aec6a9e7bf3b5ecd432f68a878264f896023ba.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503942 :question/problem "![](images/55/55ac7d0e79f21610191ebb3c861bb1a5d8e3eaf3ad625ad121e7f815bdc3c517.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the vector $a$. Which of the following diagrams shows the vector $-\\frac{2}{3}a$?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $-\\frac{2}{3}a$ must be parallel to $a$.\n\nNow, for the vector $-\\frac{2}{3}a$ we have the following:\n\n- The magnitude of $-\\frac{2}{3}a$ is $\\frac{2}{3}$ times the magnitude of $a$.\n- Vectors $a$ and $-\\frac{2}{3}a$ have opposite directions since the factor $-\\frac{2}{3}$ is negative.\n\nThe required vectors are shown below.\n\n![](images/76/76b35de153f7f4a06e5b12d3f8813fe65db408e168d03730922d2b08eb417ff7.png)"}