ages/69/69bd5a037d3d3e070c63a149145b02a1f89e769030383d438969cbf18d8d2685.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503979 :question/problem "![](images/f0/f00bdccc1b5638e9c6e2e561c3610ad7e651a3ad9b9e9370fdf2504e082ae4a3.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the vector $a$. Which of the following diagrams shows the vector $2a$?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $2a$ must be parallel to $a$.\n\nNow, for the vector $2a$ we have the following:\n\n- The magnitude of $2a$ is $2$ times the magnitude of $a$.\n- Vectors $a$ and $2a$ have the same direction since the factor $2$ is positive.\n\nThe required vectors are shown below.\n\n![](images/37/3717e8c317c7056dd705ef42bbcba9ec72658a74fde1df3ba8072ef80cc3e3b2.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503982 :question/problem "![](images/be/be7e4b00a67260420096bcd057c37bd16519369f0ef3992ab713ec8871f85dec.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the vector $a$. Which of the following diagrams shows the vector $-\\frac{4}{3}a$?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $-\\frac{4}{3}a$ must be parallel to $a$.\n\nNow, for the vector $-\\frac{4}{3}a$ we have the following:\n\n- The magnitude of $-\\frac{4}{3}a$ is $\\frac{4}{3}$ times the magnitude of $a$.\n- Vectors $a$ and $-\\frac{4}{3}a$ have opposite directions since the factor $-\\frac{4}{3}$ is negative.\n\nThe required vectors are shown below.\n\n![](images/40/40f24942ebb220e8846468f7dafa386a8cb80beac5fa41f027ea4a4846946d82.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503986 :question/problem "![](images/13/13f82a9730b605e4f4879c8db24a54a76971c025522a02cfb9152d5dbb857792.png)\n\nThe vector $a$ is depicted above. Which of the following diagrams shows the vectors $3a$ and $-\\frac{2}{3}a$?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that both $3a$ and $-\\frac{2}{3}a$ must be parallel to $a$.\n\nNow, for the vector $3a$ we have the following:\n\n- The magnitude of $3a$ is $3$ times the magnitude of $a$.\n- Vectors $a$ and $3a$ have the same direction since the factor $3$ is positive.\n\nNext, for the vector $-\\frac{2}{3}a$ we have the following:\n\n- The magnitude of $-\\frac{2}{3}a$ is $\\frac{2}{3}$ times the magnitude of $a$.\n- Vectors $a$ and $-\\frac{2}{3}a$ have opposite directions since the factor $-\\frac{2}{3}$ is negative.\n\nThe required vectors are shown below.\n\n![](images/ed/edd51a0c9f55adb319a4ea46c976574a7fde67fa2cc4432a96b6d4f7cd1b401a.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503992 :question/problem "![](images/d7/d7edbe7aef482a93fb7428ec859214ca823e11014a4b574b17fd838cb89bf05b.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the vector $a$. Which of the following diagrams shows the vector $3a$?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $3a$ must be parallel to $a$.\n\nNow, for the vector $3a$ we have the following:\n\n- The magnitude of $3a$ is $3$ times the magnitude of $a$.\n- Vectors $a$ and $3a$ have the same direction since the factor $3$ is positive.\n\nThe required vectors are shown below.\n\n![](images/38/384ae5cb00e14e98d588811cab27f850fe7fe8c24f039af1da595615b2b5abe0.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503999 :question/problem "![](images/99/99cf956064fc76c3cc164c4bbfabbb41cc97b59b6311b87df57e67e07678de33.png)\n\nThe vector $a$ is depicted above. Which of the following diagrams shows the vectors $3a$ and $-\\frac{4}{3}a$?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that both $3a$ and $-\\frac{4}{3}a$ must be parallel to $a$.\n\nNow, for the vector $3a$ we have the following:\n\n- The magnitude of $3a$ is $3$ times the magnitude of $a$.\n- Vectors $a$ and $3a$ have the same direction since the factor $3$ is positive.\n\nNext, for the vector $-\\frac{4}{3}a$ we have the following:\n\n- The magnitude of $-\\frac{4}{3}a$ is $\\frac{4}{3}$ times the magnitude of $a$.\n- Vectors $a$ and $-\\frac{4}{3}a$ have opposite directions since the factor $-\\frac{4}{3}$ is negative.\n\nThe required vectors are shown below.\n\n![](images/a9/a94a6839583f12ad5c451795544c396c5b354fdc6d7f8464f8063cf3e6b62639.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504007 :question/problem "![](images/d1/d13d2a7f949a37da5a0618dfcd2f64a4127c89831a6a2127331606ab714d44a3.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the vector $a$. Which of the following diagrams shows the vector $2a$?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $2a$ must be parallel to $a$.\n\nNow, for the vector $2a$ we have the following:\n\n- The magnitude of $2a$ is $2$ times the magnitude of $a$.\n- Vectors $a$ and $2a$ have the same direction since the factor $2$ is positive.\n\nThe required vectors are shown below.\n\n![](images/d8/d8704d00ec5e48f44782f89b3e1b7c0cb0b458285ae41a8dabe4ebc9e40ab05e.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504021 :question/problem "![](images/6e/6eabcbd1bb3ee4c019704fae93cefe9c4e2d98cc76aed83c9530c53e2086fd10.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the vector $a$. Which of the following diagrams shows the vector $4a$?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $4a$ must be parallel to $a$.\n\nNow, for the vector $4a$ we have the following:\n\n- The magnitude of $4a$ is $4$ times the magnitude of $a$.\n- Vectors $a$ and $4a$ have the same direction since the factor $4$ is positive.\n\nThe required vectors are shown below.\n\n![](images/4f/4fd95161ecee991b1d7b8c3ef3510010915bd08a4126719b8a750b93b51d0032.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504655 :question/problem "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)={e}^{2y}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2})$ increase most rapidly at the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{2},0)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{2y}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2}))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{2y}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2}))\\,j \\\\  & =2x{e}^{2y}{sec}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})\\,i+2{e}^{2y}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2})\\,j \\\\ ∇f(\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{2},0) & =2\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{2}\\right){e}^{0}{sec}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\,i+2{e}^{0}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\,j \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,i+2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $2\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,i+2\\,j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504745 :question/problem "Given that $λ(5a - 25b)$ and $6a - 30b$ are equal (non-zero) vectors, find the scale factor $λ$." :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n$$\nλ(5a - 25b)=λ(5)a+λ(-25)b.\n$$\n\nThe same scale factor must multiply the whole non-zero vector. Comparing the two coefficients gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ &= \\frac{6}{5} = \\frac{6}{5} \\\\\nλ &= \\frac{-30}{-25} = \\frac{6}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n6a - 30b=\\frac{6}{5}(5a - 25b).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nλ=\\frac{6}{5}\n$$\n\nThe two non-zero vectors are parallel. Their directions are the same because the factor is positive."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504750 :question/problem "Given that $-8a - 10b$ and $λ(20a + 25b)$ are equal (non-zero) vectors, find the scale factor $λ$." :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n$$\nλ(20a + 25b)=λ(20)a+λ(25)b.\n$$\n\nThe same scale factor must multiply the whole non-zero vector. Comparing the two coefficients gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ &= \\frac{-8}{20} = -\\frac{2}{5} \\\\\nλ &= \\frac{-10}{25} = -\\frac{2}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n-8a - 10b=-\\frac{2}{5}(20a + 25b).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nλ=-\\frac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nThe two non-zero vectors are parallel. Their directions are opposite because the factor is negative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504753 :question/problem "Given that $-3a - 24b$ and $λ(0.5a + 4b)$ are equal (non-zero) vectors, find the scale factor $λ$." :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n$$\nλ(\\frac{1}{2}a + 4b)=λ\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)a+λ(4)b.\n$$\n\nThe same scale factor must multiply the whole non-zero vector. Comparing the two coefficients gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ &= \\frac{-3}{\\frac{1}{2}} = -6 \\\\\nλ &= \\frac{-24}{4} = -6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n-3a - 24b=-6(\\frac{1}{2}a + 4b).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nλ=-6\n$$\n\nThe two non-zero vectors are parallel. Their directions are opposite because the factor is negative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504768 :question/problem "Given that $λ(0.25a - 3b)$ and $-a + 12b$ are equal (non-zero) vectors, find the scale factor $λ$." :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n$$\nλ(\\frac{1}{4}a - 3b)=λ\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)a+λ(-3)b.\n$$\n\nThe same scale factor must multiply the whole non-zero vector. Comparing the two coefficients gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ &= \\frac{-1}{\\frac{1}{4}} = -4 \\\\\nλ &= \\frac{12}{-3} = -4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n-a + 12b=-4(\\frac{1}{4}a - 3b).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nλ=-4\n$$\n\nThe two non-zero vectors are parallel. Their directions are opposite because the factor is negative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504773 :question/problem "Given that $18a + 45b$ and $λ(4a + 10b)$ are equal (non-zero) vectors, find the scale factor $λ$." :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n$$\nλ(4a + 10b)=λ(4)a+λ(10)b.\n$$\n\nThe same scale factor must multiply the whole non-zero vector. Comparing the two coefficients gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ &= \\frac{18}{4} = \\frac{9}{2} \\\\\nλ &= \\frac{45}{10} = \\frac{9}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n18a + 45b=\\frac{9}{2}(4a + 10b).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nλ=\\frac{9}{2}\n$$\n\nThe two non-zero vectors are parallel. Their directions are the same because the factor is positive."}]}
